Let $(f_n)_{n\ge1}$ be a sequence in $X^*$ with $0<a\le\|f_n\|\le b<\infty$. Suppose that, for each $m\ge1$, a finite set $D_m\subset B_X$ satisfies $$\label{ev:normers}
 \max_{d\in D_m}|v(d)|\ge\tfrac12\|v\|
 \quad\bigl(v\in\operatorname{span}\{f_1,\ldots,f_m\}\bigr),
 \qquad f_n(D_m)=\{0\}\quad(n>m).$$ Then $(f_n)$ is a basic sequence whose prefix projections have norm at most $2$, and its coefficients satisfy $$\label{ev:coefficients}
 |c_n|\le\frac4a\left\|\sum_i c_i f_i\right\|
 \quad\text{for every finite sum.}$$ Put $G=\overline{\operatorname{span}}\{f_n:n\ge1\}$, let $f_n^\#$ be its basis coefficient functionals, and put $Z=\overline{\operatorname{span}}\{f_n^\#:n\ge1\}\subset G^*$. If $R_G(X)\subset Z$, then $R_G:X\to Z$ is onto. More precisely, its adjoint satisfies $$\|R_G^*h\|\ge\tfrac12\|h\|\qquad(h\in Z^*).$$

Let $X$ and $E$ satisfy the hypotheses of Lemma <a href="#pos:density" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="pos:density">7</a>. There are $\varepsilon>0$ and a family $(h_\alpha)_{\alpha<\kappa}$ in $S_{E^{**}}$ such that the functionals $f_\alpha=h_\alpha\circ R_E\in X^*$ satisfy $$\label{pos:seminormalized}
 \varepsilon<\|f_\alpha\|\le1\qquad(\alpha<\kappa),$$ and, for every $y\in R_E(B_X)$, $$\label{pos:pointwise-null}
 \mu\{\alpha:h_\alpha(y)\ne0\}=0.$$

Distinct infinite paths in the same family have only finitely many weights in common. Consequently, for any finitely many distinct paths $P_1,\ldots,P_h$ in that family, one may delete finitely many initial pieces of each to obtain tails $\widetilde P_1,\ldots,
\widetilde P_h$ satisfying $$\left\|\sum_{b=1}^{h}a_b\widetilde P_b\right\|_{E^*}
 \le\|(a_b)_{b=1}^{h}\|_{\ell_q}
 \qquad(a_b\in\mathbb R).
 \label{path:tail-estimate}$$ Each difference $P_b-\widetilde P_b$ belongs to $X_0$.
